{"type":"say","text":"Thanks for that critical information, Rosa. That changes things significantly. If the brazed joints can't go above 120 degrees Celsius, then powder coating is absolutely off the table — I won't risk compromising the structural integrity of this frame. Low-temperature rust-inhibiting enamel is the right call here.

So here's where I land on your proposal, Evan: I can accept the trade — three jars of your homemade blackberry preserves, no cash — for stripping and repainting the frame with low-temperature rust-inhibiting enamel. I'll also need to treat that visible surface rust as part of the prep work. Sunday at 14:00 pickup works fine with my open weekend.

Before I finalize anything on the job card, I want to make sure we're all on the same page: the finish will be enamel only, cured at or below 120 degrees Celsius, and the surface rust will be addressed during stripping. Does that sound right to everyone?"}

